  "title": "CONCLUSION AND FUTURE WORK",
  "content": [
    {
      "id": "text",
      "text": "Across 50 studies and 38 quantitative effect sizes, green walls in tropical and warm climates produced a mean exterior surface temperature reduction of 4.7 Â°C (95% CI: 3.2â6.1) and HVAC energy savings of 18.4% (95% CI: 12.7â24.1, trim-and-fill corrected to 15.7%). The strongest single moderator wasn't climate or orientation â it was substrate moisture, which absorbed roughly 38% of between-study variance and which most studies still don't report continuously. Modular living walls outperformed direct climbers by more than 3 Â°C on average, and west-facing installations beat north-facing by over 80%. The evidence supports green walls as a credible passive cooling strategy for hot-humid contexts, with the caveat that the largest effects appear under conditions (well-irrigated modular systems on west faÃ§ades) that require commitment to maintenance most building owners under-budget. Three honest limitations deserve flagging. First, publication bias inflates the energy-savings estimate; the trim-and-fill-corrected 15.7% is a more defensible upper bound. Second, north-facing walls and direct-climber typologies have thin evidence â n = 9 and n = 6 respectively â and conclusions there are tentative. Third, only 11 studies cover a full annual cycle, so seasonal-shoulder effects are under-quantified. Future work should: (1) standardise continuous substrate-moisture logging as an extraction variable in primary studies; (2) re-run costâbenefit analysis [44] with tropical labour rates and cooling-degree-day profiles, since payback estimates from Mediterranean contexts probably misprice the tropics; (3) build mechanistic models that decompose the ÎT into shading, evapotranspiration, and added thermal mass components â current empirical estimates lump these together, which limits design transferability."
